From: QCRemote
Subject: [PATCH] drivers: usb: uhc_dwc2: start periodic channels whose frame has passed

port_start_periodic() only starts a waiting interrupt channel when the next
frame number equals its scheduled frame exactly. SOF events are coalesced, so
the driver thread can run after that frame has gone by; the channel then
waits for the frame counter to wrap (16384 frames, ~16.4 s at Full Speed).
Seen on an ESP32-S3 (Zephyr v4.5.0) polling a Full Speed interrupt IN
endpoint with bInterval 1: no IN data for ~16.4 s after every connect.

Start any channel whose scheduled frame is the next frame or already
behind, moving it up to the next frame.

---
 drivers/usb/uhc/uhc_dwc2.c | 15 +++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/uhc/uhc_dwc2.c b/drivers/usb/uhc/uhc_dwc2.c
--- a/drivers/usb/uhc/uhc_dwc2.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/uhc/uhc_dwc2.c
@@ -1664,8 +1664,19 @@ static void port_start_periodic(const struct device *dev)
 			continue;
 		}

-		if (next_frame == ch->data->scheduled_frame) {
-			LOG_DBG("TODO: Channel%d schedule periodic to frame=%u",
+		const uint16_t delta = (ch->data->scheduled_frame - next_frame) &
+				       PERIODIC_FRAME_MASK;
+
+		/*
+		 * SOF events are coalesced, so this thread can run after the
+		 * scheduled frame has gone by. Waiting for an exact match then
+		 * stalls the endpoint for a whole frame-counter wrap (16384
+		 * frames, ~16.4 s at Full Speed). Start any channel whose frame
+		 * is now or already behind, moved up to the next frame.
+		 */
+		if (delta == 0U || delta >= PERIODIC_FRAME_HALF) {
+			ch->data->scheduled_frame = next_frame;
+			LOG_DBG("Channel%d schedule periodic to frame=%u",
 				ch->index,
 				ch->data->scheduled_frame);
 			ch->data->periodic_scheduled = false;
